{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And Yitzchak sent Yaakov.</b> One should ask: since he went with their commission and they had forgiven him, why was he punished regarding the commandment of honoring father and mother, when he was delayed twenty-two years — for Yosef was separated from him twenty-two years corresponding to them? And it is possible to say that their intention was that he should take Leah and return immediately when they would send for him; but he set his eyes upon Rachel the younger for her beauty, and said (Genesis 29): ״אעבדך שבע שנים ברחל״ — and if so, all the time of the delay was of his own will, and therefore he was punished corresponding to it:" "<b>And he went to Paddan-aram, unto Lavan son of Betuel the Aramean.</b> His intention in going there was in order to marry one of the daughters of Lavan, and because of this it says 'unto Lavan,' for so his father commanded him: ״קום לך פדנה ארם ביתה בתואל אבי אמך וקח לך משם אשה מבנות לבן אחי אמך״ — and as for what it says ״אחי רבקה אם יעקב ועשו״ — were we not already aware that Lavan is the brother of Rebecca, and Rebecca the mother of Jacob and Esau?" "<b>Rather,</b> by way of the plain sense (peshat), the verse intended here to honor Jacob, and to say: that Jacob went to intermarry with a wicked man, son of a wicked man — as it says ״אל לבן בן בתואל הארמי״ — do not be amazed at this, for he is the brother of Rebecca, the brother of the righteous woman, and the daughters are worthy of resembling their father's sister. Afterward it added praise of Rebecca, that she was the mother of Jacob and Esau; and it was fitting for her to love both of them equally, for they are her sons whom she bore. Moreover, it was fitting for her to love Esau more than Jacob, for such is the way of the nations with the firstborn son; and despite this, Rebecca did not set the essence of her love except upon Jacob, because she saw the beauty of his character traits, in the manner of what is written (Genesis 25:28): ״ורבקה אוהבת את יעקב״." "<b>And from the mouth</b> of the Rav, my teacher, I heard that the addition of ״אם יעקב ועשו״ — the verse intended to say that Rebecca's sending him to the house of her brother Lavan was done with calculation, because it is the place where the closeness of kinship applies equally to both of them, and he does not want one to harm the other — for she was mother of Jacob and Esau — and Lavan too would not agree that Jacob harm Esau if he goes there; all the more so he would not agree that Esau harm Jacob who comes to him under the shadow of his roof:" "<b>And by way of</b> the wisdom of nature, ״אם יעקב ועשו״: it is known that two sons born as twins each have their own amniotic sac (כאו\"א — כל אחד ואחד), but Jacob and Esau were a great wonder, for the formation of both of them was in a single placenta (שליא אחת), and because of this there was the struggling within the womb, in the manner of what is written (Genesis 25:22): ״ויתרוצצו הבנים בקרבה״ — and because of this it was necessary to say ״אחי רבקה אם יעקב ועשו״, to tell whose patience (סובלנית) this was:"], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {3 "The phrase כי אם יעקב ועשו היתה appears textually difficult; the translation reads היתה as referring back to רבקה, with כי אם taken as 'for she was.' The preceding זל״ז is read as זה לזה ('one to the other').", 4 "The word סובלנית ('patience/forbearance') is unexpected in context; translated hyper-literally. The sense may be 'whose endurance this was,' referring to Rebecca's bearing the struggling twins."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:15:52.775495677Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}